    *Note (Keny C):* The license files were not provided in the original repository. The problem was raised [here](https://github.com/ReTeX/ReX/issues/39). Given lack of reply, I'm not sure which parts of the original code were licensed by what.

That statement comes from the original author. Christopher Breeden added it to
the original repository's README (https://github.com/ReTeX/ReX, commit
ed07d57, 2016-12-17, "add license to README"), naming LICENSE-MIT and
LICENSE-APACHE files that were never committed. It is the Rust project's
standard dual-licence wording. Of the two licences it offers, oxiroot-rex takes
MIT, whose terms are reproduced above, and applies them to the original code
under this notice:

    Copyright (c) 2016-2017 Christopher Breeden
    Copyright (c) 2017-2022 Sebastian Köln and the other contributors to the
    original ReX

The sentence does not say which "portions [are] covered by various BSD-like
licenses", and no file in the source carries a licence or copyright header of
its own. Contributions made to the fork (2023 onwards) are MIT, per the fork's
README above.

The `min!`/`max!` macros in src/macros.rs were borrowed, per the upstream
source comment, from the min_max_macros crate
(https://gitlab.com/EClaesson/min_max_macros; the comment spells it
"max_min_macros"). That crate is published under the MIT license:

    Copyright (c) 2016 Emanuel Claesson

and the MIT permission notice and disclaimer reproduced above apply to it.


